A great accomplishment of our modern legal system is that it limits cycles of violence. Bloody feuds that last generations are now a thing of the past. But in return for turning human disputes into orderly proceedings, courts sometimes sacrifice the emotional dimensions of crime and punishment. People who are wronged don't just want perpetrators to be punished. Many want an acknowledgement of the harms done to them. They want offenders to truly understand the harm they have caused. They want apologies that are heartfelt, not coerced.
